1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,078
Lesson 5.4.

2
00:00:02,458 --> 00:00:05,283
Continuous stochastic processing units.

3
00:00:06,417 --> 00:00:08,708
Pbits sample binary variables.

4
00:00:09,088 --> 00:00:12,324
But many artificial
intelligence problems are

5
00:00:12,324 --> 00:00:16,496
continuous: Gaussians, linear
systems, curvature matrices.

6
00:00:16,876 --> 00:00:20,252
Normal Computing proposed
another kind of thermodynamic

7
00:00:20,252 --> 00:00:23,935
hardware, the stochastic
processing unit, built from coupled

8
00:00:23,935 --> 00:00:27,250
electrical oscillators that
jiggle from thermal noise.

9
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:30,799
Its final state contains the
solution of a linear problem.

10
00:00:31,917 --> 00:00:36,689
Each cell is a circuit with an
inductor, a capacitor and a resistor.

11
00:00:37,069 --> 00:00:41,242
The capacitor voltage oscillates,
the resistor damps it, and the

12
00:00:41,242 --> 00:00:45,089
same resistor, by Nyquist's
theorem, injects thermal noise.

13
00:00:45,469 --> 00:00:48,983
The dynamics is exactly
the underdamped Langevin

14
00:00:48,983 --> 00:00:52,863
equation from lesson 2.2,
with a quadratic potential.

15
00:00:53,243 --> 00:00:56,588
Without coupling, each
cell samples a Gaussian.

16
00:00:57,717 --> 00:01:00,847
Now we connect the cells
with coupling capacitors.

17
00:01:01,227 --> 00:01:06,901
The total energy becomes U
= ½ xᵀ A x − bᵀ x, where the

18
00:01:06,901 --> 00:01:11,854
matrix A encodes the
capacitances and couplings,

19
00:01:12,234 --> 00:01:13,849
and b are bias voltages.

20
00:01:14,229 --> 00:01:19,227
The system relaxes toward the
Boltzmann distribution of that

21
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:24,225
energy: a Gaussian with mean
A⁻¹ b and covariance k_B T A⁻¹.

22
00:01:24,605 --> 00:01:27,828
By measuring the voltages and
time-averaging, we read the

23
00:01:27,828 --> 00:01:31,222
solution of the linear system
and the inverse of the matrix.

24
00:01:32,350 --> 00:01:36,247
Let's simulate eight coupled cells
with a well-conditioned matrix.

25
00:01:36,627 --> 00:01:39,581
The relative error of the
estimated covariance is

26
00:01:39,581 --> 00:01:42,775
twenty-nine percent after
a hundred time units, eight

27
00:01:42,775 --> 00:01:46,573
percent after a thousand, and
five percent after four thousand.

28
00:01:46,953 --> 00:01:49,860
It falls as one over
the square root of the

29
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:52,700
integration time, as lesson 2.6 predicted.

30
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:57,469
Precision is bought with time, and
time is set by the condition number.

31
00:01:58,583 --> 00:02:01,359
Normal Computing's first prototype was a

32
00:02:01,359 --> 00:02:04,621
printed circuit board
with eight coupled cells.

33
00:02:05,001 --> 00:02:06,910
With it, Gaussian sampling and the

34
00:02:06,910 --> 00:02:09,491
inversion of small
matrices were demonstrated.

35
00:02:09,871 --> 00:02:13,115
The next step is an
integrated chip, where time

36
00:02:13,115 --> 00:02:16,841
constants drop to nanoseconds
and many more cells fit.

37
00:02:17,221 --> 00:02:20,859
In unit six we will see the
algorithms and their complexity, and

38
00:02:20,859 --> 00:02:24,041
in unit eight, how to use
them for the natural gradient.

39
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:26,365
An order of magnitude.

40
00:02:26,745 --> 00:02:30,220
With a one-microhenry
inductor and a one-nanofarad

41
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:34,181
capacitor, the natural frequency
is about five megahertz.

42
00:02:34,561 --> 00:02:37,951
If relaxation takes a few
periods and we need a thousand

43
00:02:37,951 --> 00:02:41,462
relaxation times for a
five-percent error, the computation

44
00:02:41,462 --> 00:02:44,428
takes on the order of a
millisecond on the board.

45
00:02:44,808 --> 00:02:47,546
On a chip, with femtofarad
capacitances, the

46
00:02:47,546 --> 00:02:49,972
same computation drops to microseconds.

47
00:02:51,083 --> 00:02:55,961
A stochastic processing unit solves
linear systems, inverts matrices,

48
00:02:55,961 --> 00:03:00,839
samples Gaussians and, as we will
see, computes the natural gradient.

49
00:03:01,219 --> 00:03:04,332
Its limits are analog
precision, mismatch between

50
00:03:04,332 --> 00:03:08,015
components and the cost of
converting voltages to numbers.

51
00:03:09,117 --> 00:03:12,555
Common mistakes: forgetting
that the matrix must be positive

52
00:03:12,555 --> 00:03:15,535
definite, because otherwise
the circuit is unstable.

53
00:03:15,915 --> 00:03:19,086
Averaging from the start without
discarding the transient.

54
00:03:19,466 --> 00:03:22,037
Expecting the precision
of a digital processor.

55
00:03:22,417 --> 00:03:25,964
And ignoring the cost of loading
the matrix and reading the voltages.

56
00:03:27,083 --> 00:03:31,443
In summary: coupled RLC
oscillators implement Langevin in a

57
00:03:31,443 --> 00:03:36,394
quadratic potential, with the
thermal noise of their own resistors.

58
00:03:36,774 --> 00:03:40,705
Their stationary state is a
Gaussian with mean A⁻¹ b and

59
00:03:40,705 --> 00:03:45,056
covariance A⁻¹, and time-averaging
gives those quantities with

60
00:03:45,056 --> 00:03:49,127
an error proportional to one
over the square root of time.

61
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:53,642
To practice: compute the
natural frequency of a cell.

62
00:03:54,022 --> 00:03:56,299
Explain the double role of the resistor.

63
00:03:56,679 --> 00:03:59,484
Estimate the time for a two-percent error.

64
00:03:59,864 --> 00:04:03,293
And describe what happens if the
matrix has a negative eigenvalue.
